      en: "Ulcinj is one of the oldest and most atmospheric cities on the Montenegrin coast, boasting a history that dates back over 2,000 years. Known for its distinct oriental flair, the city reflects centuries of Ottoman influence through its architecture, minarets, and vibrant local markets. The historic Old Town (Stari Grad) is perched on a dramatic cliff overlooking the Adriatic, with stone walls that once sheltered a notorious pirate stronghold during the 17th century. Legend has it that the Spanish author Miguel de Cervantes was once imprisoned here, drawing inspiration for his characters from the local surroundings. Beyond its history, Ulcinj is famous for \"Velika Plaža\" (the Great Beach), which stretches for over 12 kilometers and is the longest sandy beach on the eastern Adriatic coast. This natural wonder is a popular destination for sunseekers and kitesurfers alike. Additionally, the nearby Solana Ulcinj salt pans provide a vital sanctuary for migratory birds, including flamingos, making it a key site for eco-tourism in Montenegro.",
